<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tukulti-Ninurta I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1243–1207 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tukulti-Ninurta I was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> in the thirteenth century BCE, one of the most powerful rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian</a> period. Accounts of the Assyrian Empire emphasise his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> around 1225 BCE and his personal capture of the Kassite king. He also founded a new royal city, Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tukulti-ninurta-i.jpg" alt="Tukulti-Ninurta I" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail._Assyrian_king_Tukulti-Ninurta_I_stands_and_kneels,_13th_century_BCE._From_Assur,_Iraq._Pergamon_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Tukulti-Ninurta I succeeded his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-i">Shalmaneser I</a>. He won a major victory against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> at the Battle of Nihriya in the first half of his reign and took Hittite territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. He kept Assyrian control of Urartu. He is the first king known to use the title King of Kings.</p><p>He later defeated Kaštiliašu IV, the Kassite king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. According to his own account he took Kaštiliašu captive single-handed, trod with his feet upon the king's lordly neck as though it were a footstool, and deported him in chains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. He demolished the walls of Babylon, massacred many of its inhabitants and carried off the Statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a> temple. He set himself up as king of Babylon and took the ancient title King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, first used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>.</p><p>A letter found at Dūr-Katlimmu, addressed to his grand vizier Ashur-iddin, reports the captive king's journey into exile with his wife and a large retinue. Tukulti-Ninurta also defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a>, who had coveted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and an epic poem, the Tukulti-Ninurta Epic, records his wars against Babylon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. After a Babylonian revolt he plundered the temples of Babylon, an act regarded as sacrilege by all Mesopotamians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> included.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Relations with the priesthood in Aššur deteriorated, and the king built a new capital, Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta. His sons rebelled against him and besieged him there, and he was murdered during the siege. One of them, Ashur-nadin-apli, succeeded him on the throne. After his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> passed through a brief period of stagnation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tukulti-Ninurta I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kassite kings of Babylon placed a governor on the island in the fifteenth and fourteenth centuries BCE, and the Middle Assyrian king <b>Tukulti-Ninurta I</b> (r. c. 1243–1207 BCE) styled himself king of Dilmun and Meluhha, a claim probably more boastful than real.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Giorgi Melikishvili and Donald Rayfield read Colchis into Assyrian inscriptions: <b>Tukulti-Ninurta I</b> (1243–1207 BCE) defeated forty kings of Nairi and of 'the countries on the shores of the Upper Sea', and Tiglath-Pileser I (1114–1076 BCE) called himself conqueror of Nairi 'from Gilḫi to the Upper Sea', Gilḫi being…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tukulti-Ninurta I</b> of the Assyrian Empire sacked Babylon around 1225 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kassite-babylonia">Kassite Babylonia</a></li></ul></section>
